  "cited_scales": {
    "kind": "cited scales applied to the computed differences; the scales are rules of thumb of their authors",
    "note": "Quoted from the two sources, which were read for this module. Neither scale is a probability that a model is true.",
    "aic_difference": {
      "source": "Burnham and Anderson (2004), 'Multimodel Inference: Understanding AIC and BIC in Model Selection', Sociological Methods & Research 33(2), p. 271",
      "quantity": "Delta_i = AIC_i - AIC_min, for a model i of the candidate set",
      "bands": [
        {
          "from": 0,
          "to": 2,
          "label": "substantial support"
        },
        {
          "from": 4,
          "to": 7,
          "label": "considerably less support"
        },
        {
          "from": 10,
          "to": null,
          "label": "essentially no support"
        }
      ],
      "between_bands": "between the bands the source names",
      "what_the_source_says": "It calls these 'simple rules of thumb' and 'rough guidelines'; Delta_i measures how plausible it is that fitted model i is 'the best approximating model in the candidate set'. On p. 279, discussing BIC, it adds that observing a posterior model probability p_t near 1 'does not justify an inference that model g_t is truth (such a statistical inference requires an a priori certainty that the true model is in the model set)'."
    },
    "twice_log_bayes_factor": {
      "source": "Kass and Raftery (1995), 'Bayes Factors', Journal of the American Statistical Association 90(430), p. 777",
      "quantity": "2 ln(B10), the Bayes factor for hypothesis 1 against hypothesis 0; a BIC difference is a rough approximation to it (p. 778)",
      "bands": [
        {
          "from": 0,
          "to": 2,
          "label": "not worth more than a bare mention"
        },
        {
          "from": 2,
          "to": 6,
          "label": "positive"
        },
        {
          "from": 6,
          "to": 10,
          "label": "strong"
        },
        {
          "from": 10,
          "to": null,
          "label": "very strong"
        }
      ],
      "between_bands": "not applicable: the bands are contiguous",
      "what_the_source_says": "'these categories are not a calibration of the Bayes factor, but rather a rough descriptive statement about standards of evidence in scientific investigation' (p. 777). The Schwarz criterion 'may be viewed as a rough approximation to the logarithm of the Bayes factor'; minus twice it 'is often called the Bayesian information criterion (BIC)'; 'the relative error of exp(S) in approximating B12 is generally O(1)' (p. 778)."
    },
